1
00:00:11,760 --> 00:00:15,079
 Hey guys, welcome back to our listening course.

2
00:00:15,400 --> 00:00:23,900
We have journeyed together through the social conversations of parts
1 and 2, and we've navigated the academic discussions of part 3.

3
00:00:24,420 --> 00:00:30,740
But today we arrive at the destination, the final challenge, the IELTS listening part 4.

4
00:00:31,540 --> 00:00:34,720
So what is waiting for us in this part of the test?

5
00:00:35,100 --> 00:00:37,680
We will find out together in this lesson.

6
00:00:38,260 --> 00:00:40,120
This lesson aims to help you.

7
00:00:40,560 --> 00:00:48,760
First, understand the overview of the IELTS listening part four, highlighting exactly how it is different from the previous parts.

8
00:00:49,300 --> 00:00:58,280
Second, learn how to prepare for this part of the test, specifically focusing on the vocabulary and mindset you need to survive.

9
00:01:02,710 --> 00:01:05,990
I want you to look at the question sheet on the screen right now.

10
00:01:06,570 --> 00:01:09,990
Visually, it looks surprisingly friendly, doesn't it?

11
00:01:10,710 --> 00:01:12,770
It is a note completion task.

12
00:01:13,290 --> 00:01:17,510
It has clear headings, bullet points and simple gaps to fill.

13
00:01:18,270 --> 00:01:23,390
You might look at this and think, oh, this is great. It looks exactly like part one.

14
00:01:23,810 --> 00:01:26,090
I just need to catch a noun or a number.

15
00:01:26,570 --> 00:01:27,950
Easy. But be careful.

16
00:01:28,430 --> 00:01:29,630
This is a trap.

17
00:01:30,390 --> 00:01:33,930
First of all, let's take a look at these two question sets.

18
00:01:34,490 --> 00:01:37,390
One from part one and one from part four.

19
00:01:38,310 --> 00:01:43,590
I will give you 20 seconds to spot the differences in how the information is presented.

20
00:01:44,110 --> 00:01:45,610
Don't just look at the words.

21
00:01:45,930 --> 00:01:47,430
You should look at their structuring.

22
00:01:47,830 --> 00:01:48,390
Let's do it.

23
00:02:11,570 --> 00:02:13,770
Starting from the top, they look similar.

24
00:02:14,450 --> 00:02:17,190
Both are gap filling tasks with a word limit.

25
00:02:18,070 --> 00:02:22,830
But as we look closer at the content, we can see four drastic changes.

26
00:02:23,710 --> 00:02:25,550
Number one, the format.

27
00:02:26,630 --> 00:02:29,190
Look closely at the left side, section one.

28
00:02:30,030 --> 00:02:36,050
We see fields like applicant name, Sarah, contact phone and preferred team.

29
00:02:37,050 --> 00:02:38,990
When do you see a form like this?

30
00:02:39,810 --> 00:02:45,070
You see it when two people are talking, one person asking and the other answering.

31
00:02:45,930 --> 00:02:48,950
From this, we know the general rule for part one.

32
00:02:49,450 --> 00:02:51,210
It is always a dialogue.

33
00:02:52,120 --> 00:02:56,250
It relies on turn taking, which gives your brain a moment to process.

34
00:02:57,230 --> 00:02:59,970
Now look at the right side, section four.

35
00:03:00,710 --> 00:03:02,190
Does this look like a conversation?

36
00:03:03,290 --> 00:03:11,270
No, it has headings like background information, the excavation process and significance of the find.

37
00:03:12,070 --> 00:03:16,870
This looks like a page from a textbook or a student's notebook during a class.

38
00:03:17,710 --> 00:03:21,170
This leads us to the format for the IELTS listening part four.

39
00:03:21,850 --> 00:03:22,730
It is a monologue.

40
00:03:23,530 --> 00:03:24,750
There is no Q&A.

41
00:03:25,370 --> 00:03:32,770
It is just one speaker, usually a professor, delivering a long speech for five to seven minutes without interruption.

42
00:03:33,970 --> 00:03:35,570
Number two, the context.

43
00:03:36,630 --> 00:03:38,330
Look at the vocabulary on the left.

44
00:03:39,010 --> 00:03:42,550
Volunteer, weekends, evenings, festival.

45
00:03:43,210 --> 00:03:45,650
These are words you use in daily life.

46
00:03:46,210 --> 00:03:49,990
So part one focuses on social survival English.

47
00:03:50,950 --> 00:03:55,590
It tests if you can survive in an English speaking country doing everyday tasks.

48
00:03:56,570 --> 00:03:59,790
Now contrast that with the vocabulary on the right.

49
00:04:00,810 --> 00:04:06,430
Marine archaeology, 17th century, excavation, artefacts.

50
00:04:07,090 --> 00:04:09,490
These are not words you use at the supermarket.

51
00:04:10,310 --> 00:04:14,630
These are words you hear in a university lecture hall during a history lesson.

52
00:04:15,470 --> 00:04:20,310
Therefore, the language used in part four is strictly academic English.

53
00:04:21,270 --> 00:04:26,870
You are expected to understand educational content, even if you are not an expert in that field.

54
00:04:27,610 --> 00:04:37,310
Some of the most common topics you will face include archaeology, which is simply the study of human history through digging up old sites and artefacts,

55
00:04:37,630 --> 00:04:40,390
or broader ones like history and environment.

56
00:04:41,050 --> 00:04:45,950
You don't need to be a scientist, but you must be ready for this academic style.

57
00:04:47,230 --> 00:04:49,130
Number three, the questions.

58
00:04:50,150 --> 00:04:52,910
Let's look at the questions and answers you need to find.

59
00:04:53,790 --> 00:04:56,890
In section one, question one asks for a surname.

60
00:04:57,670 --> 00:05:00,050
Question two asks for a phone number.

61
00:05:00,910 --> 00:05:04,030
Question six asks for an address number, 86.

62
00:05:05,190 --> 00:05:07,850
So part one asks for general information.

63
00:05:08,370 --> 00:05:14,710
You are listening for specific, isolated details like names, numbers or standard nouns.

64
00:05:15,650 --> 00:05:16,810
But look at section four.

65
00:05:17,670 --> 00:05:20,490
Question 32 asks for the cause of sinking.

66
00:05:21,310 --> 00:05:25,670
Question 40 asks how the discovery changed historical understanding.

67
00:05:26,590 --> 00:05:31,090
These aren't just facts, they are reasons, explanations and impacts.

68
00:05:32,030 --> 00:05:38,270
That's why in part four, we need to listen to and find the answers that are also abstract ideas.

69
00:05:39,090 --> 00:05:46,010
You aren't just catching a word, you are following the speaker's logic, their theories and the connections between ideas.

70
00:05:47,310 --> 00:05:51,370
Finally, last but not least, number four, the timing.

71
00:05:52,130 --> 00:05:54,510
Let's look at the structure of the question numbers.

72
00:05:55,190 --> 00:05:57,990
In section one, we typically see a split.

73
00:05:58,580 --> 00:06:02,450
You answer questions one to five and then the audio stops.

74
00:06:03,350 --> 00:06:05,770
You get a break to read questions six to ten.

75
00:06:06,690 --> 00:06:13,810
So in part one, you will get a short break between the two given question sets, which makes this part generally easier.

76
00:06:14,730 --> 00:06:16,410
Now look at section four.

77
00:06:17,230 --> 00:06:24,630
The headings flow directly from background to excavation to findings without any visual break or pause line.

78
00:06:25,330 --> 00:06:29,490
It flows from question 31 straight through to question 40.

79
00:06:30,530 --> 00:06:34,310
This means you must maintain intense focus for the entire duration.

80
00:06:34,990 --> 00:06:41,250
If you zone out for five seconds, you might miss the transition to the next heading and lose track completely.

81
00:06:45,520 --> 00:06:49,040
So how do we survive this continuous academic monologue?

82
00:06:49,700 --> 00:06:55,300
Now, I can hear you asking if the recording is continuous and there is not a single break.

83
00:06:55,300 --> 00:07:00,420
How do I know where the speaker is or whether or not they have moved to the next question?

84
00:07:00,940 --> 00:07:03,440
The answer lies in signposting language.

85
00:07:04,000 --> 00:07:10,600
For example, they might say, now turning to the issue of this tells you we are moving to a new section.

86
00:07:11,000 --> 00:07:15,840
Or they might say, to conclude, this warns you that the summary is coming.

87
00:07:16,400 --> 00:07:19,700
However, we will focus more deeply on these in the next video.

88
00:07:19,700 --> 00:07:25,340
On the other hand, however, knowing where you are doesn't help if you don't understand what they are saying.

89
00:07:26,060 --> 00:07:28,340
Part four relies heavily on context.

90
00:07:29,000 --> 00:07:37,800
So we are going to look at the three most common topics you will face, which are environment, nature and wildlife, and history and archaeology.

91
00:07:38,200 --> 00:07:42,920
Break them down into subtopics and identify the vocabulary you must know.

92
00:07:43,480 --> 00:07:46,760
Let's start with one of the most popular themes, environment.

93
00:07:47,760 --> 00:07:51,260
This usually focuses on global issues and the planet's health.

94
00:07:52,000 --> 00:07:58,020
The subtopics here are specific things like pollution, natural disasters or energy sources.

95
00:07:58,880 --> 00:08:07,720
Therefore, the vocabulary you need includes words like sustainability, contamination, erosion, drought or renewable energy.

96
00:08:08,860 --> 00:08:13,040
Next, we have a similar but distinct category, nature and wildlife.

97
00:08:13,770 --> 00:08:17,660
This is often confused with the environment, but it is different.

98
00:08:18,320 --> 00:08:21,900
It focuses specifically on animals, plants and ecosystems.

99
00:08:22,880 --> 00:08:30,440
You might hear a lecture about migration patterns of different species, the life cycle of a plant or food chains of a specific forest.

100
00:08:31,020 --> 00:08:37,919
And you need to recognize words like habitat, predator, hibernation, nocturnal or extinction.

101
00:08:38,640 --> 00:08:45,180
And finally, let's look at one more challenging theme, history and archaeology, which we have analyzed above.

102
00:08:46,080 --> 00:08:49,780
To sum up, this often confuses students because it is so specific.

103
00:08:50,540 --> 00:08:55,260
The subtopics usually involve ancient civilizations, meaning societies that

104
00:08:55,260 --> 00:08:59,000
existed thousands of years ago, like ancient Egypt or Rome.

105
00:08:59,740 --> 00:09:07,540
Excavations, which is simply the act of digging up the ground to find buried buildings or objects, or the details of a historical event.

106
00:09:08,400 --> 00:09:10,200
The keywords here can be quite technical.

107
00:09:11,020 --> 00:09:17,740
You will hear words like sediment, hierarchy, vartifacts, chronological order or era.

108
00:09:18,520 --> 00:09:24,820
These words might not appear as the answers due to the fact that they are quite complex and too difficult for normal students.

109
00:09:25,500 --> 00:09:32,260
However, knowing their meanings helps you understand the context much more quickly, which eventually boosts your listening score.

110
00:09:32,260 --> 00:09:35,580
However, are these the only topics you will face?

111
00:09:36,100 --> 00:09:37,440
The answer is no.

112
00:09:38,200 --> 00:09:39,400
IELTS is unpredictable.

113
00:09:40,160 --> 00:09:45,020
You may find topics about technology, space exploration or even psychology.

114
00:09:45,780 --> 00:09:48,760
Therefore, the best strategy is not just memorizing lists.

115
00:09:49,420 --> 00:09:57,840
You must continuously cultivate your knowledge by reading news and listening to English materials every day alongside your structured lessons here at PrEP.

116
00:09:58,480 --> 00:10:03,420
Combining real-world practice with our exam techniques is the fastest way to improve.

117
00:10:03,940 --> 00:10:05,220
That's it for today's lesson.

118
00:10:05,500 --> 00:10:09,700
Don't forget to do the exercises and practice what you've learned regularly.

119
00:10:10,260 --> 00:10:11,980
Goodbye and see you in the next lesson.

